<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Who this guide is for</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    This guide is for principals, heads of department, training managers and anyone deciding whether and how to bring AI tools into a school, college or training program. It turns the published evidence into a sequence of steps. The steps and checklist are our own suggestions, built on what the studies report. They are not a standard and not legal advice.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    The evidence behind it is summarised in our ebook, <a href="/resources/ai-in-education-evidence-ebook/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">AI in Education: What the Trials and Surveys Show</a>. The short version: results depend on how a tool is designed and supervised, and none of the trials we cite took place in Nepal.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 1. Write down the problem before choosing a tool</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Name one specific problem in a sentence, for example “teachers spend too long preparing worksheets” or “students in a mixed-ability class cannot get help when stuck.” If you cannot name the problem, you cannot judge the result.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Then record a baseline: hours spent per week on the task, current test results, or the number of questions students ask. You will need these numbers in Step 8.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 2. Choose the lowest-risk use first</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    The evidence points to different levels of risk for different uses. This table is our reading of the sources, not a ranking from them.
  </p>
  <div class="mb-5 overflow-x-auto"><table class="w-full text-left"><thead><tr><th class="border-b border-gray-200 py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[#6B7280]">Use</th><th class="border-b border-gray-200 py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[#6B7280]">What the sources report</th><th class="border-b border-gray-200 py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[#6B7280]">Risk to learning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Teacher drafts materials, then edits</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]"><a href="https://news.gallup.com/poll/691967/three-teachers-weekly-saving-six-weeks-year.aspx" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gallup</a>: weekly users estimate 5.9 hours saved per week; quality judged at least somewhat better by 57% to 74%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Lower, if teachers review the output</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Answering routine administrative questions</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Our own consultancy case study reports faster responses; see <a href="/blog/ai-for-education-consultancy-student-inquiry-automation-guide/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">our inquiry automation guide</a></td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Lower, if answers come from maintained information</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Student tutor with hints and structure</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]"><a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12179260/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Harvard trial</a>: gains on one short topic with a carefully designed tutor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Medium; depends on design and supervision</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Open chatbot access for students</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]"><a href="https://ideas.repec.org/a/nas/journl/v122y2025pe2422633122.html" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PNAS study</a>: 17% lower later scores after practising with an unguarded chatbot</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Higher</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    If this is your first project, our own suggestion is to start in the first two rows.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 3. Set rules in plain language</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Write a one-page policy that staff and students can read in five minutes. The <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/generative-artificial-intelligence-in-education/generative-artificial-intelligence-ai-in-education" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UK Department for Education guidance</a> is a useful example of what a government says to schools: safety should be the top priority, use should be carefully considered and assessed, and pupils should use these tools only with safeguards such as close supervision and filtering and monitoring. It also recommends that personal data is not used in generative AI tools. It is UK guidance, so check what applies in your country.
  </p>
  <ul class="mb-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li>Which tasks AI may be used for, and which it may not (for example, graded assessments).</li>
    <li>Whether students must say when they used AI.</li>
    <li>Who approves new tools.</li>
    <li>What staff must check before sharing AI-drafted material.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    The OECD reports that seven in ten teachers on average believe AI could let students present others’ work as their own, so be explicit about assessment rules.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 4. Check the data and the supplier</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    An AI tool works on whatever is typed into it. Before any student or parent data goes in, ask the supplier the questions below. They draw on the <a href="https://www.unesco.org/en/artificial-intelligence/recommendation-ethics" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of AI</a> principles of privacy protection, transparency and human oversight, which include that AI systems “do not displace ultimate human responsibility and accountability.”
  </p>
  <ul class="mb-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li>What data does the tool collect, and does it need student personal data at all?</li>
    <li>Where is the data stored, and who else can see it?</li>
    <li>What is the minimum age, and how is it enforced?</li>
    <li>Can staff switch the tool off, and what happens to stored data?</li>
    <li>How was it tested, and against what comparison?</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Our <a href="/resources/ai-governance-data-privacy-sme-whitepaper/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">AI governance checklist</a> covers more of these questions.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 5. Choose tools that teach, not just answer</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    For any student-facing tool, ask what it does when a student is stuck. The Harvard tutor used pre-written step-by-step solutions and a structure that guided students through each part of a problem. The PNAS authors describe unrestricted GPT-4 access as a “crutch.” A tool that gives hints and asks questions is closer to the first than the second.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Ask for a live demonstration with a real problem from your own curriculum, in the language your students use.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 6. Train staff before launch</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Hold a short session so that teachers know what the tool can and cannot do. The OECD names professional development as part of the infrastructure needed for fair access, and Gallup reports that benefit grows with regular use. Our own suggestion is to give teachers time to try the tool on their real tasks before students see it.
  </p>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Teach one habit above all: AI output is a first draft. A person checks it before it is used, because generative systems can produce confident but wrong content. NIST describes this as confabulation, “confidently stated but erroneous or false content” in its <a href="https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">generative AI profile</a>.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 7. Run a small pilot with a teacher in the loop</h2>
  <ul class="mb-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li>Pick one course, one group of teachers or one class.</li>
    <li>Run it for a fixed period, such as one term.</li>
    <li>Keep a teacher present when students use any AI tutor.</li>
    <li>Collect problems as they happen: wrong answers, student confusion, privacy concerns.</li>
    <li>Agree in advance what would make you stop.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    A small pilot is also the right response to the evidence gap. Without trials in Nepal, only your own data can tell you what works for your students.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Step 8. Measure learning, not only activity</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Compare against the baseline from Step 1. Include at least one check that students take without the tool. In the Turkish trial, practice performance rose with AI access, but scores fell once it was taken away. Practice scores while using the tool do not show that learning happened.
  </p>
  <ul class="mb-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li>Teacher time on the task, compared with your baseline.</li>
    <li>Student results on a test taken without AI.</li>
    <li>Number and type of errors found in AI output.</li>
    <li>Student and teacher feedback in their own words.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Then decide: stop, adjust, or expand to one more group. Avoid expanding because it felt good. Expand because the numbers moved.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Checklist before you launch</h2>
  <ul class="mb-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li>A single written problem and a recorded baseline</li>
    <li>A first use that is teacher-facing or tightly supervised</li>
    <li>A one-page policy covering allowed uses, assessment and disclosure</li>
    <li>A named person who can pause the pilot</li>
    <li>Supplier answers on data, age limits, storage and switch-off, in writing</li>
    <li>No personal data entered unless you have checked it is permitted and necessary</li>
    <li>Staff trained, and a review step for AI-drafted material</li>
    <li>A tool that gives hints and steps instead of answers, if students use it</li>
    <li>A way to test students without the tool</li>
    <li>A date to review the results and decide</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    If you would like help designing a pilot or a custom system for your institution, see our <a href="/solutions/custom-agent/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">custom AI agent development</a> or <a href="/schedule-a-call/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">schedule a call</a>.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Sources</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
